    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.edge_percolation - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.edge_percolation.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.edge_percolation()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Simulate edge percolation by randomly removing edges.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.edge_percolation()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Simulate edge percolation by randomly removing edges.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.edge_percolation()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Simulate edge percolation by randomly removing edges.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nSimulate edge percolation by randomly removing edges.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L59582-L59703){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.edge_percolation(\n    q,\n    seed=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Edge percolation](../assets/graphs/edge-percolation.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Before: the two diamonds sharing hub D. After removing 30 percent of the edges at random (here B–D, C–D, and D–E, drawn dashed), every node survives but the graph has split into the triangle A, B, C and the four nodes D, E, F, G.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nCreates a new graph with a fraction of edges removed uniformly at random. All nodes are\npreserved. This is the basic operation of *bond percolation* and models random link\nfailures: cables cut, roads closed, friendships lapsing, or interactions that fail to\ntransmit an infection. Comparing the surviving graph with the original (for example,\nwith `largest_component_size()`) shows how dependent the network's connectivity is on\nindividual links.\n\nTypical uses include:\n\n- stress-testing communication or transport networks against random link failures\n- simulating an epidemic in which each contact transmits only with some probability\n- generating perturbed versions of a graph for sensitivity analyses\n\nThe number of edges removed is `round(q * m)`, where `m` is the edge count, so the\nremoved fraction is exact up to rounding, and every edge is equally likely to be chosen.\nBecause nodes are never removed, a node can end up isolated. To remove nodes instead,\nuse `node_percolation()`.\n\nThe original graph is not modified. The returned graph keeps the directedness of the\noriginal, and nodes and surviving edges keep their IDs, labels, weights and attributes.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[q]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Fraction of edges to remove, between `0.0` and `1.0` inclusive. For example, `q=0.2`\n    removes 20% of the edges. `q=0.0` keeps every edge and `q=1.0` leaves only the\n    isolated nodes.\n\n<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Seed for the random number generator. Calls with the same graph, `q=`, and `seed=`\n    remove the same edges, which makes results reproducible. If `None` (default), a fresh\n    random seed is drawn on every call.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A new Graph containing all of the original nodes but only the surviving edges.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `q=` is outside the range `[0.0, 1.0]`.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~node_percolation`) \n\n:   Remove nodes instead of edges.\n\n[](`~percolation_threshold_analysis`) \n\n:   Track the largest component over many removal rates.\n\n[](`~node_connected_component`) \n\n:   The piece that a node is left in.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThe graph below has ten edges: two clusters (`A`, `B`, `C` and `E`, `F`, `G`) joined\nthrough the hub `D`. Removing 30% of the edges deletes three of them. With this seed they\nare `B-D`, `C-D` and `D-E`, so every node survives but the largest connected piece holds\nonly four:\n\n::: {#c128981c .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"B\", \"D\"), (\"C\", \"D\"),\n     (\"D\", \"E\"), (\"D\", \"F\"), (\"E\", \"F\"), (\"E\", \"G\"), (\"F\", \"G\")]\n)\n\npercolated = graph.edge_percolation(q=0.3, seed=14)\n\n(\n    percolated.count_edges(),\n    percolated.largest_component_size(),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(7, 4)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### How far a rumor spreads\n\nEdge percolation can model a rumor in which each tie passes it on with some chance. In\nZachary's karate club, keep 30% of the 78 friendships (`q=0.7`), and a rumor started by\nthe instructor (member 0) reaches the members in the instructor's piece of what's left.\nThat varies a lot from one draw to the next:\n\n::: {#f136aea2 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nclub = ec.karate_club().layout_kamada_kawai()\n\n[\n    len(\n        club.edge_percolation(q=0.7, seed=s).node_connected_component(\"0\")\n    )\n    for s in range(10)\n]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n[7, 21, 21, 21, 4, 18, 17, 23, 20, 17]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nIn this draw the rumor reaches seventeen members, half the club. Highlighted, they keep\ntheir faction colors, and the members it never reaches are gray:\n\n::: {#d0119305 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nrumor = club.edge_percolation(q=0.7, seed=6)\n\nrumor.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", color_by=\"faction\", label_by=\"label\",\n    highlight=sorted(rumor.node_connected_component(\"0\")),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ec